  <h1>Types of Wrongful Termination Claims</h1>

  <p>
    Wrongful termination is not a single cause of action — it is a category that encompasses several distinct legal theories, each with different elements, procedural requirements, and damages frameworks. Understanding which type of claim applies to your situation is the first step toward assessing the strength and value of your case.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Discrimination-Based Wrongful Termination</h2>
  <p>
    The most common wrongful termination claims involve employment discrimination under federal statutes: Title VII of the Civil Rights Act (race, color, religion, sex, national origin), the Age Discrimination in Employment Act (workers 40 and older), the Americans with Disabilities Act (physical and mental disabilities), and the Pregnancy Discrimination Act. Many states have parallel statutes with broader coverage — California's FEHA, for example, covers employers with 5 or more employees vs. Title VII's 15-employee threshold.
  </p>
  <p>
    To establish a discrimination claim, an employee typically must show they were a member of a protected class, they were performing their job satisfactorily, they were subjected to an adverse employment action (including termination), and the circumstances give rise to an inference of discrimination (such as being replaced by someone outside the protected class, or evidence that similarly situated employees outside the class were treated differently).
  </p>
  <p><strong>Typical settlement range:</strong> $25,000–$300,000 (federal); no cap on state law claims in many states. Median EEOC discrimination charge settlements run $40,000–$75,000, but cases with strong evidence, significant back pay, and egregious facts routinely settle for $150,000–$500,000 or more.</p>

  <h2>2. Retaliation</h2>
  <p>
    Retaliation claims arise when an employer fires an employee for engaging in legally protected activity. Protected activities include: reporting workplace harassment or discrimination to HR or the EEOC; filing a workers' compensation claim; making a complaint to OSHA about unsafe working conditions; requesting a reasonable accommodation for a disability; opposing unlawful employment practices; or participating in a government investigation or legal proceeding.
  </p>
  <p>
    Retaliation is the single most commonly filed charge at the EEOC, accounting for over 55% of all charges in recent years. Courts and juries react strongly to evidence that an employer punished an employee for exercising their rights — which tends to produce higher emotional distress awards and, in egregious cases, punitive damages.
  </p>
  <p>
    To prove retaliation, an employee generally must show: they engaged in protected activity; the employer took an adverse action against them; and there is a causal connection between the two (often established through temporal proximity — being fired shortly after the protected activity).
  </p>
  <p><strong>Typical settlement range:</strong> $50,000–$400,000. Retaliation claims with clear temporal proximity and strong documentation tend to settle at the higher end of employment case ranges.</p>

  <h2>3. FMLA Violations</h2>
  <p>
    The Family and Medical Leave Act gives eligible employees at covered employers the right to take up to 12 weeks of unpaid, job-protected leave per year for qualifying medical and family reasons. Firing an employee for taking or requesting FMLA leave, or for reasons that are pretextual cover for FMLA retaliation, constitutes a wrongful termination.
  </p>
  <p>
    FMLA claims have a powerful damages feature: for willful violations, the statute provides for liquidated damages equal to the back pay award, effectively doubling the recovery. Courts have discretion to award liquidated damages in interference claims (where the employer denied leave or failed to provide required notices) as well as retaliation claims.
  </p>
  <p>
    FMLA coverage requires that the employer have 50 or more employees within 75 miles of the employee's worksite, and the employee must have worked at least 12 months and 1,250 hours in the previous year. Some states have more generous leave laws (California's CFRA, New York PFL) that cover smaller employers and provide paid leave.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Typical settlement range:</strong> $10,000–$150,000, with willful violation cases potentially doubling the back pay component through liquidated damages.</p>

  <h2>4. Breach of Employment Contract</h2>
  <p>
    While most U.S. employees are at-will, some employees have written employment contracts that specify the grounds for termination or require a notice period or severance. If an employer fires an employee in violation of the terms of a written contract — without cause when the contract requires cause, or without required notice — the employee may have a breach of contract claim.
  </p>
  <p>
    Employee handbooks and policies can also create implied contractual obligations in some states. Courts in states like California, Michigan, and Montana have held that handbook promises of progressive discipline or "for cause" termination standards can be enforceable. The implied contract theory is highly jurisdiction-specific.
  </p>
  <p>
    Unlike discrimination claims, contract claims do not require EEOC exhaustion and can be filed directly in state court. Damages are governed by contract principles: the employee is entitled to the economic benefit they would have received had the contract been honored.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Typical settlement range:</strong> Highly variable. The contract terms define the floor (the remaining contract value) and the ceiling is typically limited to economic damages without the emotional distress and punitive components available in discrimination cases.</p>

  <h2>5. Public Policy Violations</h2>
  <p>
    Most states recognize a tort claim for wrongful discharge in violation of public policy — a common-law cause of action that fills gaps in statutory protection. An employee fired for refusing to commit a crime, for exercising a statutory right (like filing for workers' compensation or serving on jury duty), or for performing a legal obligation (like cooperating with a government investigation) may have a public policy claim even without a specific statute covering the situation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The public policy exception is narrower in some states (like New York, which requires the public policy to be embodied in a statute) and broader in others (like California and Massachusetts, which recognize the tort more expansively). It does not require EEOC exhaustion and allows for tort damages, including emotional distress and potentially punitive damages.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Typical settlement range:</strong> $30,000–$400,000. California cases under FEHA's broad public policy framework can produce multi-six-figure and seven-figure verdicts in egregious cases.</p>

  <h2>6. Whistleblower Retaliation</h2>
  <p>
    Dozens of federal and state whistleblower protection statutes protect employees who report illegal conduct, safety violations, securities fraud, environmental violations, tax fraud, or consumer financial fraud to regulatory agencies or internally. Key statutes include: the Sarbanes-Oxley Act (publicly traded company financial fraud), the Dodd-Frank Act (SEC/CFTC violations), the False Claims Act (government contractor fraud), OSHA's whistleblower protection provisions covering over 20 industries, and the IRS Whistleblower Program.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whistleblower claims often carry the strongest damages. Under Dodd-Frank, a prevailing whistleblower who reported securities violations to the SEC is entitled to double back pay plus attorney fees. The False Claims Act allows qui tam plaintiffs to recover a percentage of government funds recovered (15–30%), which can produce multi-million dollar awards in major cases. Emotional distress and punitive damages are available under many state whistleblower statutes.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Typical settlement range:</strong> $50,000–$500,000+ under standard whistleblower statutes; qui tam False Claims Act cases can reach into the millions for large-scale fraud schemes.</p>

